whenever we kill these, father and also son,
and among them you then will be slain, for the things you purpose
to do in the halls;

But when your force we have taken away with bronze,
the possessions, as many as are yours, both those within and those out of doors,
with those of Odysseus we shall mix;

And the suitors Damastor's son Agelaus urged on,
and Eurynomus and Amphimedon and Demoptolemus,
and Peisander, son of Polyctor, and Polybus, wise-minded;

but all of them to no purpose made Athena;
of them, one the doorpost of the well-standing hall
had struck, another the door, closely fitted;
